menu "SlateFPV board"

choice SLATE_BOARD
    prompt "Board"
    default SLATE_BOARD_C3_SUPERMINI if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    help
      Which physical board this image is for.

      The chip is necessary and not sufficient. Two boards carrying the same
      part can put the LED on different pins, drive it with opposite polarity,
      or have no LED at all -- so selecting a target is not the same as
      selecting a board, and only the pins below decide what gets driven.

config SLATE_BOARD_C3_SUPERMINI
    bool "ESP32-C3 Supermini"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    help
      The board SlateFPV ships and flies on. LED on GPIO8 (inverted), BOOT
      button on GPIO9. Both are strapping pins and nothing else may be wired
      to them.

config SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    bool "Other board -- set the pins below"
    help
      Any other board. Nothing is assumed about it: the LED is absent unless
      you give it a pin.

endchoice

config SLATE_LED_GPIO
    int "Status LED GPIO (-1 for none)"
    depends on SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    range -1 48
    default -1
    help
      -1 means this board has no LED we can drive, and every LED state becomes
      a no-op.

      That is the default on purpose. Several dev boards -- the S3 DevKitC and
      the C6 DevKitC among them -- put an addressable WS2812 where the C3
      Supermini has a plain LED, and this firmware drives a plain LED only.
      Pointing it at a WS2812's data pin will not light it, and pointing it at
      a pin that carries something else can do worse than nothing.

      Check your board's schematic. A dark LED is a much better failure than a
      pin driven by mistake.

config SLATE_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "LED lights when the pin is LOW"
    depends on SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    default y
    help
      True when the LED sits between 3V3 and the pin, which is the common
      arrangement on dev boards.

config SLATE_BUTTON_GPIO
    int "Button GPIO (-1 for none)"
    depends on SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    range -1 48
    default 9 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C61 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5 || IDF_TARGET_ESP32C2
    default 0
    help
      Defaults to the BOOT button, which nearly every dev board has: GPIO9 on
      the RISC-V parts, GPIO0 on the S3 and the original ESP32.

      Worth having. The button is the recovery gesture -- a ten second hold
      enters setup with no flight controller, no radio and no working camera
      link involved. Set it to -1 only if the board genuinely has no button, in
      which case setup is reachable only from the setup switch or a cable.

config SLATE_BUTTON_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "Button reads LOW when pressed"
    depends on SLATE_BOARD_GENERIC
    default y
    help
      True for a button to ground with a pull-up, which is what BOOT is.

endmenu
